e": "WBAY", "published": "2026-10-21T20:30:00+00:00", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMimAFBVV95cUxPdW5reE8zRkFJSUJObU1CWTNDdHVkRVhPeEN3b3ktMTNNTkhHclNzS1pUR2ZnR1JDREUtZHVkejBLTVp0YThMa0tBVFFFaVREUU1ZQ3hXdVlQMGVxODdvbmZ3em9EVTNjNHF1b1p3OEFHRFNnbk1GZ0I4WjU0U1dXMTUyNXlwSkFKTGxEbjhBTnhWbDdRWnVGUw?oc=5"}], "model": {"prior_margin": 2.75, "prior_sd": 5.34, "poll_margin": 3.36, "poll_sd": 6.2, "poll_weight": 0.43, "fundamentals": {"margin": 3.13, "lean": 0.0, "environment": 4.35, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": -1.22}, "rating_margin": 2.5, "sigma_race": 5.3, "sigma_total": 5.71, "baseline_margin": 3.01, "analyst_adjustment": 0.39, "key_factors": ["Polls uniformly show Crowley ahead by 1-5 points, averaging about +3.4", "Tiffany holds a roughly 10-to-1 fundraising and cash-on-hand advantage", "Open seat in an evenly divided state with a Democratic-tilting midterm environment", "Prediction markets price Crowley near 80 percent"], "rationale": "Every published poll since spring has Crowley up by one to five points, and markets price him near 80 percent, outweighing Tiffany's large cash advantage in an open, evenly divided state.", "watch": "A Tiffany-funded late advertising surge or a debate-driven shift could tighten a race where the polling lead is only low single digits.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Tony Evers (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Wisconsin's governor race is an open contest after Tony Evers chose not to seek reelection, pitting Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley against Congressman Tom Tiffany. The state's partisan lean is even, and polls have consistently placed Crowley ahead by one to five points, with an average near three and a half. Rating outlets are split between Lean D and Tossup, while prediction markets price Crowley's chances near four in five.\n\nTiffany holds a large financial edge, raising roughly ten times Crowley's total, which could fuel a late advertising push. Debate coverage centered on taxes,